WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.820
Tool calling and function calling allow an LLM integration to request structured actions from application code.

2
00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:19.800
A tool might search documents, query a database, send email, create a ticket, update a calendar, call an API, run an approved code helper, or gather context from another service.

3
00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:23.660
This is powerful because it connects language to capability.

4
00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:29.920
It is also risky because model output can influence which capability is used and with what arguments.

5
00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:34.600
Read-only and write-capable tools should be controlled differently.

6
00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:46.940
Read-only tools can still expose sensitive data, but write-capable tools can modify records, send communications, provision resources, trigger workflows, or create cost.

7
00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:54.480
Each tool should have scoped credentials, a narrow purpose, allowed actions, rate limits, and clear audit events.

8
00:00:54.580 --> 00:01:00.180
A model should not receive broad administrator authority just because it can produce a plausible request.

9
00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:02.700
Argument validation is essential.

10
00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:14.240
Tool calls should validate resource identifiers, tenant context, field values, destination addresses, sizes, formats, and business intent before execution.

11
00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:18.820
Allowlisted tools and actions make it clear what the model may request.

12
00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:22.280
Dry-run behavior lets users inspect proposed actions.

13
00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:34.860
Approval gates should be required for high-impact changes, external communications, privileged operations, or actions that affect money, access, safety, compliance, or critical workflows.

14
00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:37.940
The model should not directly become a sensitive action.

15
00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:48.840
The application should decide whether a user is authorized, whether the tool is allowed, whether arguments are safe, whether a human must approve, and how the action is logged.

16
00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:56.700
Audit trails should capture who requested the action, what the model proposed, what the application executed, and what result came back.

17
00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:02.400
That separation keeps automation useful without letting generated text bypass control.
